WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.278
A contour integral adds changes along a directed path

00:00:04.278 --> 00:00:07.724
Parameterize the path by a real variable t

00:00:07.724 --> 00:00:17.633
Multiply the function value by the complex velocity of the path, then integrate in t; both path shape and orientation are accounted for.

00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:24.123
For the function z, an antiderivative is z squared divided by two

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:29.745
Every piecewise smooth path from zero to one plus i therefore gives i

00:00:29.745 --> 00:00:35.800
The endpoint formula follows because this antiderivative exists throughout the region containing the path.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.943
Now travel counterclockwise around the unit circle while integrating one over z

00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:47.190
The path velocity is i times z, so the product simplifies to i

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.033
Integrating from zero to two pi gives two pi i, not zero.

00:00:53.333 --> 00:01:00.547
There is no contradiction with Cauchy's theorem: one over z is undefined at the enclosed origin

00:01:00.547 --> 00:01:08.212
A holomorphic function on an open region containing the whole disk has zero integral around its boundary

00:01:08.212 --> 00:01:10.467
Enclosed singularities change the situation.
